I’ll inspect the pattern, metadata, and the reusable refinement utilities first, then screen candidate phases and build the GSAS-II model in staged, auditable branches. I’ll keep the final exports synchronized with the selected project and run both project and submission audits before handing it back.

The metadata points to the Cu–W–O system heated at 800 °C, so the chemically likely inventory is copper tungstate plus possible CuO/WO₃ remnants or related oxide polymorphs. I’m now extracting the experimental peak fingerprint and querying the local COD resource by both chemistry and diffraction agreement; I’ll treat composition as a prior, not as identification.

The strongest peaks form a single coherent fingerprint of triclinic wolframite-type CuWO₄; several peaks that superficially resemble CuO or WO₃ are also expected CuWO₄ reflections. I’m testing that structural assignment first against the entire profile, then I’ll add each precursor/polymorph one at a time and require a stable positive scale plus multiple unique residual peaks before retaining it.

The ambient-cell P‑1 model aligns every major line with a smooth angle-dependent offset; once that correction is allowed, the whole-pattern residual drops sharply rather than improving only around overlaps. This is strong evidence that the apparent CuO/WO₃ “matches” are coincidental reflections of CuWO₄, but I’m still running explicit leave-one-in tests for those precursor phases after the single-phase model is mature.

The angular test converged cleanly after extending the helper’s branches: sample displacement alone is slightly better than zero-only (Rwp 15.512% vs 15.554%), while the joint branch is rejected at 0.9999 correlation. For peak shape, an independently refined Lorentzian X term is the only simple global term so far that improves the fit while keeping widths physically positive; W, U, and Y branches run to nonphysical negative-width solutions and are being rolled back.

The phase inventory is now narrowed to dominant triclinic CuWO₄ with a supported trace WO₃ component. I’m continuing with staged coordinate, profile, microstructure, and texture tests, then I’ll replay the winning model into the final GSAS-II project and audit every exported file.

The WO₃ candidate earns its place through several non-overlapping peaks, while CuO refinements collapse to zero scale. I’ve now isolated atomic-coordinate refinements to CuWO₄ only, so the trace phase cannot borrow intensity through an underdetermined coordinate model. First I’m testing Cu/W positions, then oxygen positions from that stable branch.

Coordinate refinement is chemically sound: the Cu–O and W–O contact ranges remain plausible and both CIF audits are clean. Of the newly tested Gaussian terms, refining U is rejected because the total high-angle variance becomes negative; V remains viable and improves the fit across a valid positive-width range. I’m now checking whether that gain is distributed across the pattern and whether simpler width/texture explanations supersede it.

The dominant phase shows real but modest finite-size broadening (~0.54 µm): it helps both the low- and high-angle regions, though it slightly worsens the middle. Microstrain is weaker but independently positive, so I’m running the required joint test; the WO₃ size/strain gains are negligible and will not be retained.

The simultaneous replay exposed an important redundancy: CuWO₄ isotropic size and the global Lorentzian X term correlate at 99.55%, so that branch is not defensible. I’m rolling it back and rebuilding the width model with explicitly neutral phase broadening values before retesting size and strain; this avoids treating GSAS-II’s nonzero defaults as evidence.

With neutral phase broadening, neither size nor microstrain is supported; the earlier apparent size term was entirely entangled with X. The defensible nuisance model is now global X/V, one polarization parameter, and only a very mild CuWO₄ [001] March–Dollase term. Ten background coefficients remain preferable to 12 because the extra pair gives only a marginal gain. I’m rerunning the prescribed angular branch comparison on this mature model now.

The mature angular test selects sample displacement alone (−509.5 µm); the joint zero/displacement branch is rejected at 0.99993 correlation. A fixed-cell CuO retest gives only 0.18 wt% and ΔRwp ≈0.0015, while its apparent larger benefit requires implausible cell drift, so CuO remains excluded. I’m completing the near-tied WO₃ polymorph replay before freezing the phase assignment.

The final-width retest found one additional supported term: Gaussian U. In a simultaneous replay it lowers Rwp from 6.923% to 6.640%, all total widths remain positive, and U/V are significant but correlated at 0.92—high enough to track, below the rejection threshold. I’m comparing the simpler U-only alternative and rechecking the remaining profile terms before rerunning the angular selector.

The U/V/X/polarization/[001]-texture terms all survive matched leave-one-out tests; removing them raises Rwp to 6.923, 7.004, 8.885, 7.978, and 6.680%, respectively. Removing WO₃ raises it to 7.221%. Final size/strain retests again return neutral or negative values, so no phase broadening is retained. The selected model is now converged; I’m auditing residuals, fractions, geometry, and cross-file consistency before export.

Identified crystalline phases:

- CuWO₄, triclinic `P -1`: **97.864 wt%**
- WO₃, monoclinic `P 21/c`: **2.136 wt%**

